---
/**
 * Your own baseline, one writer at a time — and what SDNN is not. Rendered by
 * /science/sleep-heart/night-hrv; see scienceTopics.ts.
 *
 * Laid out as the canvas board (WebNightHRV): the two methods side by side,
 * each with what it is, what it is for and what is against it; then who
 * writes HRV to Apple Health; then the three reasons in three columns:
 * night only, your own usual, no score. The wearables table is checked
 * against each vendor's own pages before every release of the app; a row
 * that cannot be confirmed is removed rather than left to age.
 */
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import { NIGHT_HRV_SECTION } from "../../data/science";
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";
const rhr = topicHref("resting-heart-rate");
const hrr = topicHref("heart-rate-recovery");
const sleep = topicHref("sleep-rhythm");

/** The two methods, side by side: what each is, what it is for, and what is against it. */
const methods: Array<{ name: string; what: string; pro: string; con: string }> = [
  {
    name: "SDNN",
    what: "The standard deviation of all the beat intervals in the window: how widely the beats are spread around their own average. It takes in every rhythm present, fast and slow.",
    pro: "The clinical standard over 24 hours, the definition of Apple Health's one HRV field, and what Apple Watch measures and writes.",
    con: "The slow rhythms need time to show, so its value depends on how long the window is: a 1-minute SDNN and a 24-hour SDNN are different quantities with one name. Over Apple's windows of about 1 minute a single sample is noisy; a night's median of them tames that.",
  },
  {
    name: "rMSSD",
    what: "The root mean square of the differences between each beat and the next: how much the interval jumps from one beat to the one after.",
    pro: "Only a fast rhythm can move a beat against its neighbour, and the fast rhythm at rest is breathing, steered by the vagus nerve, so it is close to a pure read of vagal tone, barely cares how long the window is, and is steadier in short windows. The research favourite for athlete monitoring, and the ring makers' choice.",
    con: "It needs clean beats: a single missed or doubled beat is a large jump, and the squaring makes it larger. And it is not what Apple's field means: a ring's number and a watch's are not the same quantity, so they must never share a baseline.",
  },
];

const wearables: Array<[string, string, string, string]> = [
  ["Apple Watch", "SDNN", "Yes, as SDNN", "Samples of about 1 minute, several a night; Verve takes the median of the ones inside the sleep window."],
  ["Oura", "rMSSD", "No HRV, per third-party reports", "Sleep and resting rate reach Apple Health; the HRV stays in the Oura app."],
  ["WHOOP", "rMSSD", "No HRV, per third-party reports", "WHOOP's stated reason: writing rMSSD into a field defined as SDNN would mislead."],
  ["Garmin", "rMSSD", "No HRV, per third-party reports", "Garmin's own status reads rMSSD over the night; it is not written to Health."],
  ["HRV4Training, Elite HRV", "rMSSD-based", "Yes, into the SDNN field", "A chest-strap or camera reading, written by the app under Apple's one HRV type."],
];

/** The three reasons, in three columns. */
const reasons: Array<{ head: string; body: string }> = [
  {
    head: "Why night only",
    body: "A watch samples HRV through the day, and the daytime samples are contaminated: standing up, talking, a flight of stairs and a coffee all change the interval between beats for reasons that have nothing to do with the nervous system at rest; the reading then says more about the minute than about you. The night is the one stretch when the body is still, the breathing is regular and the sympathetic drive is low, so Verve keeps the samples that fall inside the night's sleep window and takes their median. A median, not a mean, so one sample taken during a turn in bed does not drag the night with it.",
  },
  {
    head: "Why your own usual",
    body: "HRV spans roughly a fivefold range between healthy people of the same age, and it falls with age in everyone, so a population band would mislabel most of them: a “normal” of 20 to 100 ms says nothing about whether your 34 is good. The only fair line is your own: the median of your last 30 nights with a reading, and the middle 50% of those nights as the band. Whether your 34 is your usual 34 or a fall from your usual 48 is the only question the number can answer, and it is the same rule resting heart rate is read by.",
  },
  {
    head: "Why no score",
    body: "Readiness scores fold HRV, sleep and resting rate into one number by a formula the vendor does not publish, with a cheerful word under it; there is no study behind the fold. Verve shows the three readings and names a pattern only when all three leave their own bands in the same direction, HRV low, resting rate high, recovery small, for 3 nights. A single low night is a night: a late meal, a drink, heat, a hard session and the first day of a cold all do this, and the number cannot tell them apart.",
  },
];
---

<p class="mt-[18px] text-base leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  Verve reads whatever HRV reaches Apple Health, names the writer and its
  method, and keeps each writer&rsquo;s baseline apart. Here is why that
  matters. Heart rate variability is the beat-to-beat unevenness of a resting
  heart: a heart that speeds a little on each breath in and slows on each
  breath out is being steered by the vagus nerve, and the more freely it is
  steered, the larger the variation. That is what the number measures, and
  it is all it measures. It is not a fitness score, and it is not a readiness
  score, whatever the dashboard beside it says.
</p>

<!-- the two methods, side by side -->
<div class="mt-5 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {methods.map((m) => (
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-4 sm:p-5">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">{m.name}</p>
      <div class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-2 text-[12.5px] leading-[18px] text-text-secondary">
        <p><strong class="text-text-primary">What it is.</strong> {m.what}</p>
        <p><strong class="text-text-primary">For.</strong> {m.pro}</p>
        <p><strong class="text-text-primary">Against.</strong> {m.con}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  ))}
</div>
<p class="mt-3 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
  At night, at rest, with the sympathetic drive low, the two track each other
  closely, because both are then dominated by the same thing: the
  breathing-driven vagal variation. For a personal baseline neither is
  &ldquo;better&rdquo;, provided the two are never mixed on one line: an
  rMSSD of 60 and an SDNN of 60 are not the same night, and a chart that holds
  both is a chart of the switch between them.
</p>

<!-- who writes it -->
<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">
    Who writes HRV to Apple Health
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/20">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[620px] text-[12.5px]">
      <thead>
        <tr class="text-left font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-text-tertiary">
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Wearable</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Its HRV method</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Writes HRV to Apple Health?</th>
          <th class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold">Note</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/[0.06]">
        {wearables.map(([w, m, writes, note]) => (
          <tr class="align-top">
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-semibold text-text-primary">{w}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 font-mono text-text-secondary">{m}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-secondary">{writes}</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-text-tertiary">{note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    Apple Health has one HRV type, and its definition is SDNN. Apple Watch
    writes SDNN into it. Oura, WHOOP and Garmin compute rMSSD and, per
    third-party reports, do not write HRV to Apple Health at all. Some
    chest-strap and camera apps write rMSSD-based values into the SDNN
    field, which is why Verve reads the writer&rsquo;s name as well as the
    number.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    <strong class="text-text-secondary">So is there anything to choose?</strong>
    Usually not. With an Apple Watch and any of Oura, WHOOP or Garmin, the
    Watch is the only writer of HRV, and Verve&rsquo;s Preferred device row
    for night HRV simply says so. The one exception is a chest-strap or
    camera app such as HRV4Training or Elite HRV, which writes its own
    rMSSD-based number into the same field. Then two writers exist, and
    Settings &rarr; Apple Health &rarr; Preferred device lets you name
    which leads. Each writer keeps its own baseline either way.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[10.5px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    Vendor behaviour changes; this table is checked against the vendors&rsquo;
    Apple Health pages before each release.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- the three reasons -->
<div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px] lg:grid-cols-3">
  {reasons.map((r) => (
    <div class="text-[12.5px] leading-[18px] text-text-secondary">
      <strong class="text-text-primary">{r.head}.</strong>
      {" "}{r.body}
    </div>
  ))}
</div>

<!-- how a night is read, and Verve's rules -->
<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">
    How a night is read
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {[
      ["The reading", "the median of the samples inside the night's sleep window, ms"],
      ["The usual", "the median of your last 30 nights, from the same writer"],
      ["The band", "the middle 50% of those 30 nights"],
      ["The mean", "the average of your last 7 nights, drawn through them; shown from 3 nights, settled at 7"],
      ["The scatter", "a week's standard deviation as a share of its mean; read against your own prior weeks"],
      ["The card", "the writer and its method: Apple Watch · SDNN"],
      ["What it is not", "a population figure, a readiness score, a verdict"],
    ].map(([k, v]) => (
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-0.5 rounded-xl border border-white/8 bg-white/[0.03] px-3 py-2.5">
        <span class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-text-tertiary">{k}</span>
        <span class="text-[12.5px] text-text-secondary">{v}</span>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Verve&rsquo;s rules.</strong>
    One writer per night: a night with samples from two sources is read
    from one of them, never averaged across both. The writer and its method
    are printed on the card, &ldquo;Apple Watch · SDNN&rdquo;, or
    &ldquo;HRV4Training · rMSSD&rdquo;, or &ldquo;method undeclared&rdquo;
    where the writer does not say. The baseline is kept per writer, so a new
    writer starts a new band from its own first night rather than being
    judged against the old writer&rsquo;s. Two methods never share a line.
    The sleep window is the one the <a href={sleep}>sleep timing</a> page
    describes.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">When the Guide says anything at all.</strong>
    The Guide names a pattern only when night HRV,
    <a href={rhr}>resting heart rate</a> and
    <a href={hrr}>one-minute recovery</a> all leave their own bands in the
    same direction, HRV low, resting rate high, recovery small, for 3 nights.
    Three readings agreeing for three nights is a pattern worth a sentence.
    One reading on one night is not, and the Guide stays quiet.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- the two readings beside the night: the 7-night mean, and the week's scatter -->
<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">
    Two readings beside the night
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    A single night is noise. The athlete-monitoring literature acts on two
    numbers instead, and from October 2026 Verve shows both. The first is the
    <strong class="text-text-primary">7-night mean</strong>: every night, the
    last seven nights averaged, drawn as a line through the nights on the Day
    view and read against the same band as the night itself. It is shown from
    3 nights, the minimum Plews found could stand in for a week, and marked
    settled at 7. The second is the
    <strong class="text-text-primary">week&rsquo;s scatter</strong>: the
    standard deviation of a fixed week&rsquo;s nights as a share of their
    mean, the coefficient of variation, printed on the Week and Month views
    beside the period before.
  </p>
  <figure class="mt-5 space-y-4">
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30">
      <img
        src="/science/night-hrv-seven-night-mean.png"
        alt="Sixty nights of HRV as dots, the 7-night mean as a line through them, and a shaded band for the middle half of the 30 nights before. One low night barely moves the line. Four low nights in a row pull it below the band, where it turns red."
        width="1456"
        height="740"
        class="block w-full"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <figcaption class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
      <strong class="font-semibold text-text-secondary">One night is noise; seven are a signal.</strong>
      Each dot is a night. The shaded strip is this person&rsquo;s own usual
      range, the middle half of the 30 nights before. The line is the 7-night
      mean. The single dot that falls to 29 barely moves it. Four low nights in
      a row pull it below the band, and that is the pattern worth noticing.
      Illustrative data; the method is the one the app runs on your nights.
    </figcaption>

    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30">
      <img
        src="/science/night-hrv-mean-and-scatter.png"
        alt="Four weeks of seven nights each. A baseline week with a mean of 45 and scatter of 3 percent; a hard week with the same mean and scatter of 9 percent; an overreached week with the mean fallen to 38 and scatter of 10 percent; an adapted week with the mean risen to 50 and scatter back to 3 percent."
        width="1456"
        height="680"
        class="block w-full"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <figcaption class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
      <strong class="font-semibold text-text-secondary">The mean and the scatter are two signals.</strong>
      Scatter is how much the nights bounce around their own average; it looks
      inside one week and never compares weeks. In a hard week the mean holds
      and the scatter widens. If the load stays and the nights stay uneven, the
      mean falls. So the scatter is the early warning and a falling mean the
      confirmation. Verve grades a week&rsquo;s scatter only against your own
      prior weeks, never against a population figure. Illustrative values after
      Esco et al, Sensors 2025.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p class="mt-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    On Android, Health Connect&rsquo;s one HRV type is defined as rMSSD, the
    opposite choice to Apple&rsquo;s. Verve&rsquo;s Android build does not
    read it yet; when it does, the same rules apply, and an Android rMSSD
    baseline will never be drawn on an Apple SDNN line.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {NIGHT_HRV_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
</div>
